# An AUTO-07p continuation played through xppautX --script (docs/protocol.md
# "Scripts"). Run: xppautX --script lecar_auto.jsonl lecar.ode
#
# The Le Car model's ODE file defines four named parameter sets (its
# "set NAME {...}" blocks); "hopf" is the one whose parameters actually put
# a Hopf bifurcation on the branch as iapp varies, so this script selects it
# first, finds the fixed point there by Newton (Sing pts / Go) and imports
# it as the initial condition, so AUTO starts from a converged point.
# Every "answer" below omits "id": it always answers whichever ask is
# pending, which is what lets a script like this one be written without
# running it first to learn the ids.

# File / get a parameter set -> the Param set menu -> pick "hopf"
{"cmd":"key","key":"f"}
{"cmd":"key","key":"g"}
{"cmd":"answer","key":"d"}

# Sing pts / Go: a Newton search from the current initial condition
{"cmd":"key","key":"s"}
{"cmd":"answer","key":"g"}
# "Print eigenvalues?" -- no
{"cmd":"answer","key":"n"}
# the equilibrium window's Import: the point just found becomes the IC
{"cmd":"eqimport"}

# File / Auto: open the AUTO window
{"cmd":"key","key":"f"}
{"cmd":"key","key":"a"}

# Run -> steady state, from the converged fixed point
{"cmd":"auto","op":"run"}
{"cmd":"answer","key":"s"}

# Grab the first label on the diagram: Tab cycles the candidates, Return
# picks the one shown
{"cmd":"auto","op":"grab"}
{"cmd":"answer","key":"Tab"}
{"cmd":"answer","key":"Return"}

# The grabbed point is a Hopf bifurcation, so Run now offers its own menu;
# Periodic starts the periodic branch bifurcating from it
{"cmd":"auto","op":"run"}
{"cmd":"answer","key":"p"}

# File / Save diagram
{"cmd":"auto","op":"file"}
{"cmd":"answer","key":"s"}
{"cmd":"answer","file":"lecar.auto"}
